ave the  more you can learn  the education ministry has promised to  provide 30  000 computers to at least improve the  hardware problem  but that won't be enough for all many  families like the villa roes  will have to continue finding their own  solutions to ensure their children can  take part in online schooling  until the vaccine rollout advances  enough for children to return to the  classroom  should schools remain open or should  they be closed to stop the spread of the  virus  let's bring in jonathan suk a principal  expert in emergency preparedness at the  european center  for disease prevention and control in  stockholm  good to have you with us and i know that  you conducted a study into covit 19 in  schools  when it comes to keeping schools open or  not what are your findings  thank you very much for having us first  of all we're we're constantly updating  um the literature and we're reviewing uh  the studies on this  we're finding that of course any like  any uh what we call  non-pharmaceutical interventions if you  close something in society that leads to  less  social mixing so it will have an impact  on the overall uh  transmission as well as cov2 so it's  potentially important to measure what  we're seeing  and what the most recent literature  shows is that  on the second wave and more recent  closures of schools have less of an  impact than they did in the first wave  and this has to do with  schools have adopted in the way that  they've been practicing they're not  operating as they did in pre-pandemic  levels but they're doing more measures  internally to to keep them  safe to keep students safe to try to  keep teachers safe  and those measures have an impact and so  we think that  it's it may be necessary to close  schools there's no question about that  with the new variant circulating  but the general contestants that uh  debacho unisa vsdc have  relates to trying to keep schools open  um and closing them only as a last  resort first of all  could you define that last i mean who or  what determines what  this last resort would be  that has to be decision makers in the  local context to understand the  transmission levels  understand the burden on the healthcare  system of the current levels of icu and  so on  and who really can do a proper  assessment of what measures are in place  in schools  if everything has been tried within the  schools to really keep your transmission  down  or not and then what sorts of levels of  schools let's also not  make it a binary thing schools do not  need to be just fully open or fully  closed they're hybrid models  they're different age groups that are  important to consider so it's not just a  whole  either or situation in most cases either  there's a lot of options there for  authority makers doesn't  necessarily make it more easy to to uh  come to the right conclusion  uh do we actually know where children  catch the virus in the first place  is that happening at school do they get  from teachers from other children or do  they  bring the virus into the school because  they catch it at home  that's a fantastic question and we've  been trying to  understand that better in terms of the  school settings themselves  what we've actually found is that there  are very few documented instances where  you see large  outbreaks in school settings so  transmission certainly happens  in schools but but the literature from  many different countries in europe and  globally suggests that  there's not necessarily the need for  lots of outbreaks in school settings  what we wonder and what we need to learn  more about of course are what happens  to children not just in the school  itself but after school extracurricular  activities  on the way to school on the way home  from school and of course as you  mentioned in household  settings there's also the possibility  for transmission so you don't have  precise numbers on that but the the  information on on schools is that they  can actually be operated safely without  too many  instances of of large outbreaks i mean  this  these still quite trying and very  confusing times a lot of parents  feel safer having their kids around and  uh  some in some countries where schools  reopen decided to not  send their kids back to school because  they don't trust the situation do they  have a point  it's really hard to to assess that on  without knowing the specific  details of the context in which uh they  have made those  decisions i certainly understand why  people are worried  there's a lot of research also from from  germany a large study just from scotland  recently that shows at least for the the  parents of younger children  there does not appear to be an increased  risk to to the parents  catching the virus from from their  children even at periods when they've  been sent back to to school  we also know that by and large the the  number of  severe outcomes in children is very very  low um  we're not seeing any change in that even  with the new  variants we've been following very  closely dated millions of cases and  and children particularly younger  children follow this age  the age gradient so we know that elderly  continue to be  the most risk followed by by older  adults but children  really have very very rare instances of  severe outcomes such as hospitalization  or death  right but we do know that children can  carry  and they can spread the virus so what do  you make of the concept of homeschooling  wouldn't that in this  moment in time make most sense  yeah it's it's true that children can  can transmit the virus  as i mentioned it appears to to be the  case that they transmitted somewhat less  than adults do and they appear also to  be less susceptible to the virus than  adults do i'm by no means an expert in  education and homeschooling but i what  we've understood  from the vast amount of literature that  exists that it is very difficult for  children to to be home  and we understand as well that there is  a gradient so the so  learning loss from children is less for  in children from  from well-off backgrounds but from  deprived backgrounds  vulnerable backgrounds this can be  really detrimental to the the education  and even the health and well-being of  children so all these things have to be  taken into account  there's no easy answer i really  appreciate that but but these are the  things that  decision makers do need to consider  indeed a lot of food  for thought there for decision makers  jonathan zuck from the european  center for disease prevention and  control in stockholm  thank you so much for your time happy  easter thank you very much  thank you well in time for your  questions now over to derek  how long can vaccine side effects last  does discomfort indicate it's working  i want to leave all the the speculation  about blood clotting  and the astrazeneca vaccine behind for a  minute  and focus on what we know for certain  about  common minor side effects reported  in connection with covet vaccines pretty  much  all of the ones approved so far in in  various parts of the world  appear to be pretty reactogenic which  means  they regularly cause mild side effects  like  like pain and swelling at the injection  site as well as things like like fatigue  headache fever chills  or aching joints the single shot  johnson johnson vaccine appears to cause  those effects  somewhat less often in two-dose  mrna vaccines they seem to occur  more commonly after the second dose  while the opposite seems to hold true  for the astrazeneca vaccine  another point worth mentioning is that  that younger people  seem to react more often and more  powerfully  than the elderly it's thought that's  because they have  more responsive immune systems  authorities  say the symptoms can last a few days  but but in most of the first-hand  reports that i read  they subsided within 36 hours  the good news is that side effects while  unpleasant  are actually a sign that the vaccine is  doing  what it's supposed to do which is laying  the groundwork  for an immune response so that if you  are infected with the virus  your body can get to work straight away  wiping it out  um personally i'm more than happy to  experience  a day or two of discomfort if i know  that i'll be protected afterwards  from a potentially life-threatening case  of covet 19.  that sounds like a really great deal to  me  and finally as shopping malls are closed  amid pandemic lockdowns  a school in the u.s state of vermont  decided to make use of the empty space  students can now take lessons in this  form of macy's more why not  well that's all for now thanks for  watching

